WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:01.320
-: Okay, so there is more

00:01.320 --> 00:03.460
to using Git Stash and I'm gonna show it

00:04.436 --> 00:06.413
to you throughout the rest of this section.

00:06.413 --> 00:09.570
But I'm gonna be honest upfront, at least in my experience

00:09.570 --> 00:12.420
90 something percent of the time I use anything

00:12.420 --> 00:14.790
with the stash or with stashing

00:14.790 --> 00:18.450
it's just git stash and git stash pop.

00:18.450 --> 00:21.000
So those are the two things I use the most.

00:21.000 --> 00:23.910
Put something in the stash and pop it when I need it.

00:23.910 --> 00:24.840
However

00:24.840 --> 00:28.950
we can also use something called apply get stash apply

00:28.950 --> 00:31.140
which will take whatever's in the stash

00:31.140 --> 00:34.728
and apply it in the same way that POP does except

00:34.728 --> 00:39.420
with Apply the stash stays in the stash, or in other words

00:39.420 --> 00:42.464
the changes are still in the stash and we can apply them

00:42.464 --> 00:45.330
in multiple places versus with pop, our changes are removed

00:45.330 --> 00:47.610
from the stash after they're applied.

00:47.610 --> 00:49.560
So I'll demonstrate this.

00:49.560 --> 00:50.940
This could be useful

00:50.940 --> 00:53.610
if you wanted to have some stash changes that you

00:53.610 --> 00:56.910
need to apply in multiple places across different branches.

00:56.910 --> 00:59.640
I'm just gonna come up with a pretty contrived example.

00:59.640 --> 01:02.260
I guess all of the examples so far have been

01:03.240 --> 01:05.880
so we're on this goodbye branch.

01:05.880 --> 01:07.210
If I type get status

01:08.785 --> 01:10.868
we have these changes, the goodbye changes, right?

01:10.868 --> 01:12.840
We can see that here, goodbye world.

01:12.840 --> 01:14.760
But I haven't added or committed them

01:14.760 --> 01:17.133
so I could just stash them again.

01:17.133 --> 01:18.120
Git stash.

01:18.120 --> 01:20.130
Alright, now we type Git status

01:20.130 --> 01:22.500
and everything's clean working tree clean.

01:22.500 --> 01:24.840
And if I refresh the page

01:24.840 --> 01:27.480
we see it says Hello world now, right?

01:27.480 --> 01:30.810
It doesn't say goodbye, it doesn't say goodbye here

01:30.810 --> 01:33.390
and we don't have that magenta background.

01:33.390 --> 01:36.930
Okay, so I have those changes stashed away

01:36.930 --> 01:39.450
and I can go to a different branch.

01:39.450 --> 01:42.360
Should we go to master get switched master?

01:42.360 --> 01:44.670
Let's see what are we working with here?

01:44.670 --> 01:48.390
Sure. So I'm on master now and if I wanted to

01:48.390 --> 01:50.999
apply the changes that are in the stash

01:50.999 --> 01:54.150
which makes the background magenta of the body

01:54.150 --> 01:56.850
and it also changes this to say goodbye world

01:56.850 --> 01:59.370
and the title to say goodbye.

01:59.370 --> 02:02.340
I can do git stash pop, that would be one option.

02:02.340 --> 02:04.890
But if I also wanna apply this somewhere else

02:04.890 --> 02:09.840
I can do git stash apply and we get a conflict.

02:09.840 --> 02:12.570
You can see here it says auto merging index

02:12.570 --> 02:14.880
H T M L merge Conflict in index.

02:14.880 --> 02:17.790
So now it's complaining to me just like any other merge

02:17.790 --> 02:20.850
we've done, the difference is that here we applied something

02:20.850 --> 02:23.160
from the stash and that generated the conflict

02:23.160 --> 02:25.980
versus merging a specific branch.

02:25.980 --> 02:27.300
But it works the exact same way.

02:27.300 --> 02:30.030
I need to decide, well which one of these do I want?

02:30.030 --> 02:32.220
Do I want hello or goodbye?

02:32.220 --> 02:34.680
So I'll keep the changes that are coming from the stash.

02:34.680 --> 02:38.370
So accept that incoming change, accept this incoming change.

02:38.370 --> 02:40.680
I'm gonna save in the c s s file.

02:40.680 --> 02:43.260
We have that magenta color and now I refresh

02:43.260 --> 02:45.180
and we see goodbye world.

02:45.180 --> 02:46.800
Okay, great.

02:46.800 --> 02:49.617
And I could add and commit that if I wanted to

02:49.617 --> 02:53.285
I could add both of those files, get commit

02:53.285 --> 02:58.285
let's just say, you know, merge stashed changes

02:59.130 --> 03:00.933
Or let's say apply.

03:03.780 --> 03:06.900
So we have to make a commit because there was a conflict.

03:06.900 --> 03:10.350
But if I go back to the other branch, what was it called?

03:10.350 --> 03:11.370
I can't remember now.

03:11.370 --> 03:14.100
It's to git branch, yes.

03:14.100 --> 03:16.050
Goodbye. If I go back to goodbye.

03:16.050 --> 03:18.723
So git switch, goodbye.

03:20.520 --> 03:23.850
Our CS file is empty and we're back to hello.

03:23.850 --> 03:25.950
Right? We didn't make a commit with that stuff

03:25.950 --> 03:29.010
but I still have those changes stashed away

03:29.010 --> 03:33.210
because I didn't use pop, git stash, pop, pops those changes

03:33.210 --> 03:36.300
off of the stash, they're gone and they're applied.

03:36.300 --> 03:38.460
But if I use git stash apply

03:38.460 --> 03:41.190
it's still there so I can apply it again.

03:41.190 --> 03:43.350
Git stash apply.

03:43.350 --> 03:44.250
And there we are.

03:44.250 --> 03:46.350
We now have those changes back in here.

03:46.350 --> 03:48.960
App c s s has magenta

03:48.960 --> 03:51.780
and now we have goodbye and goodbye world

03:51.780 --> 03:54.000
and I'll refresh the webpage and there it is.

03:54.000 --> 03:55.920
So I know it's not the most exciting thing ever

03:55.920 --> 03:59.310
but that is using apply instead of pop.

03:59.310 --> 04:01.560
This can come in handy if you need to apply changes

04:01.560 --> 04:05.400
across multiple branches, compare that to pop git stash pop

04:05.400 --> 04:07.560
which removes the changes from the stash.

04:07.560 --> 04:10.443
Apply, keeps them in there, but also applies them.
